<div class="calcpad-output">
 <h3>Scroll Anchoring Test - #UI with an asynchronously rendered drawing</h3>
 <p>
  Every #UI edit re-renders the whole document. The drawing below is not part of that
  <p>render: its script imports a viewer from a CDN, draws to a canvas and only makes the</p>
  <p>image visible once all of that has finished - seconds after the page has loaded. Until</p>
  <p>then the image occupies no height at all.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <b>What to check.</b>
  Scroll so a line of the filler text sits exactly at the top of
  <p>the preview, then change one of the inputs. That line must stay where it is, both</p>
  <p>
   through the re-render and at the moment the drawing appears. Changing
   <b>Height</b>
  </p>
  <p>changes the drawing aspect ratio, so the image comes back a different height - the</p>
  <p>case a remembered scroll offset can never get right.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h3>Inputs above the drawing</h3>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  Width
  <span class="eq">
   <var>width</var>
   = 10
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  Height
  <span class="eq">
   <var>height</var>
   = 8
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  Image width (px)
  <span class="eq">
   <var>imgWidth</var>
   = 800
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  Border colour
  <span class="eq">
   <var>border</var>
   = 1
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h3>Filler</h3>
 <p>
  Enough text above the drawing that the top of the viewport lands in the middle of it,
  <p>and enough below that the page can be scrolled past the drawing entirely.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
